{
  "about": "Shared vectors for the dependency audit (swamp-club#3352 push, swamp-club#3353 scorecard, swamp-club#3354 and swamp-club#3682 scoring). Copy verbatim; the swamp copy is the source. `resolution`: the version that ships for a specifier; `locked` lists every version a lockfile records for the package, `lockedFor` the version it records for this specifier itself. `findings`: the exact findings push reports for one specifier, given the registry's answer; `scorecardMessage`, where present, is the scorecard's wording of the same finding. Each expected finding's `pointsLost` is what it alone costs the rubric's 2-point dependency-trust factor: 2 for every error, `not-audited`, `deprecated-version` and `jsr-yanked`, 1 for `loose-specifier`, 0 otherwise. Push only warns on the warning causes. `scoring`: the factor's earned points (0, 1 or 2) for a combination of findings: any finding costing 2 gives 0; otherwise any loose finding gives 1, however many; otherwise 2. `loose-specifier` findings count per distinct dependency. `installed`, on a resolution or record vector, is the version a package.json project holds in node_modules (swamp-club#3630). `record`: the dependency-audit.json entry push writes for one import (swamp-club#3662); `source` says how the source wrote it (`written` as npm:/jsr:, `import-map` as a bare import the Deno config resolves, `package-json` as a bare import resolved to the installed package), `spec` is the version the audit reads (for `package-json`, the range package.json declares, or the installed version when it declares none), and `expect` is the entry, or null when none is written. The entry's `spec` is what the shipped source writes after `@`: package.json-mode imports ship pinned to the installed version. `recordEntries`: whether the registry uses a record entry as the version that ships (`use`) or ignores it and resolves the dependency itself (`ignore`): it uses one only when the registry is npm or jsr, the version is a plain semver (no `v` or `=`), the spec admits it (an exact spec names it, a range contains it, a dist-tag or no spec admits any version), and `published` lists it.",
